  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\n\n17Z synopsis shows a large longwave trough across the northern plans \nwith a dampening upper level high moving to the west across the \ndesert southwest.  This is leading to upper level flow more out of a \nwesterly direction.  A 700 mb shortwave is developing in eastern \nColorado with a surface low in northeast New Mexico.  A frontal \nboundary extends from near Elkhart to St. John and a few isolated \nstorms have developed from Dodge City to Great Bend.\n\nTonight will have the best opportunity for some widespread rain in \nsouthwest Kansas.  Short term models forecast the frontal boundary \nsagging south into northern Oklahoma and the shortwave will move out \nof Colorado into southwest Kansas providing lift along and north of \nthe front.  Severe weather is expected to be limited to some \nisolated wind gusts as shear values remain low for hail production \nand DCAPE values will be around 1000-1100 J/kg.  Areas of heavier \nrainfall are possible as the storms will be developing and moving in \na training fashion through the night.  NBM probabilities of greater \nthan 0.5 inch of rain a 24 hr period ending early Thursday afternoon \nare 60-80% for areas along and south of the K-96 corridor.  Further \nnorth away from the front the rain chances go down as Hays to \nWaKeeney should expect much less rainfall.\n\nThursday will be noticeably cooler with departing storms and clouds \nin the morning combining with more north to northeast winds through \nthe day allowing for the slight cold air advection all day.  Highs \nare forecast to be in the 80s and NBM probabilities of highs being \nless than 90 degree for Thursday are 90-100% across southwest \nKansas.  Friday the upper level winds stay out of a west-northwest \ndirection and the surface winds switch back to the southwest which \nwill bring in the return of hotter temperatures.  By Friday evening \ninto Saturday morning the medium term models are showing a shortwave \nand cold front moving into central Kansas and bringing back some POP \nchances (20-30%) mainly in our eastern zones.\n\nWeekend temperature trends have changed a bit.  Long term ensembles \nhave a glancing blow of cooler air on Saturday which should drop \nhighs into the 80s and 90s and then surface winds switch back to the \nsouthwest on Sunday heating us up into the mid 90s.  Early next week \nensembles have a stronger longwave trough in the northern plains \nwhich will bring cooler air into the northern plains for early next \nweek.",
